1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
UK Biobank is a shared research project that

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:05,600
began recruiting in 2006.

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:08,760
Half a million volunteers in Britain, aged 40

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:11,760
to 69, gave blood samples and agreed to

5
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,360
link their health records.

6
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:17,840
Each sample was tested at around 850,000 places

7
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:20,560
in the DNA, and approved researchers around the

8
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:22,760
world can analyse the data.

9
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,520
So what can data from half a million

10
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:28,720
people show that one family, or one hospital,

11
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:30,000
never could?

12
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:31,760
And how far can we trust it?

13
00:00:32,967 --> 00:00:35,766
A project like this is called a large-scale

14
00:00:35,847 --> 00:00:39,686
collaborative project: many researchers and sites pool their

15
00:00:39,767 --> 00:00:40,246
data.

16
00:00:40,887 --> 00:00:44,326
Scale matters because most variants only nudge risk

17
00:00:44,407 --> 00:00:44,966
a little.

18
00:00:45,447 --> 00:00:47,926
In a small study, chance alone can make

19
00:00:48,007 --> 00:00:50,726
one group look different, so a small real

20
00:00:50,807 --> 00:00:52,646
effect gets lost in the noise.

21
00:00:53,207 --> 00:00:55,846
With hundreds of thousands of people, a real

22
00:00:55,927 --> 00:00:57,846
difference stands out from chance.

23
00:00:58,327 --> 00:01:01,766
That gives stronger inference, meaning a conclusion drawn

24
00:01:01,847 --> 00:01:02,566
from evidence.

25
00:01:03,047 --> 00:01:05,126
Stronger, but never certain.

26
00:01:06,334 --> 00:01:08,013
Pooling data isn't new.

27
00:01:08,414 --> 00:01:09,693
Here's the classic case.

28
00:01:10,174 --> 00:01:13,053
The sickle cell allele causes sickle cell anaemia

29
00:01:13,214 --> 00:01:15,133
when someone inherits two copies.

30
00:01:15,694 --> 00:01:18,493
A harmful allele like that should become rare,

31
00:01:18,574 --> 00:01:20,413
because natural selection removes it.

32
00:01:21,054 --> 00:01:23,453
But when frequency data from many countries were

33
00:01:23,694 --> 00:01:26,173
combined into one map, the allele turned out

34
00:01:26,254 --> 00:01:28,973
to be common across large parts of Africa,

35
00:01:29,014 --> 00:01:31,453
and also found around the Mediterranean, the Middle

36
00:01:31,534 --> 00:01:32,573
East and India.

37
00:01:33,294 --> 00:01:35,773
A pattern that a simple model can't explain

38
00:01:36,094 --> 00:01:38,813
is exactly what makes scientists dig deeper.

39
00:01:40,034 --> 00:01:42,993
Studies like this combine three kinds of evidence.

40
00:01:43,474 --> 00:01:47,313
Family trees show how it's inherited: unaffected parents

41
00:01:47,394 --> 00:01:50,593
can have an affected child, so it's recessive,

42
00:01:50,674 --> 00:01:52,033
and carriers are healthy.

43
00:01:52,674 --> 00:01:56,033
DNA data show what changed: one base in

44
00:01:56,074 --> 00:01:57,153
the haemoglobin gene.

45
00:01:57,634 --> 00:02:00,033
And frequency data show where it's common.

46
00:02:00,514 --> 00:02:03,073
No single kind answers the whole question.

47
00:02:03,394 --> 00:02:06,273
The conclusion comes from all three agreeing.

48
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:10,140
So why is it common in those places?

49
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:12,540
Lay a map of malaria over the allele

50
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:15,860
map, and across Africa, they line up closely.

51
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:18,340
Malaria is caused by a parasite that a

52
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:21,340
mosquito passes on, and it grows inside red

53
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:22,300
blood cells.

54
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:25,500
In carriers, infected cells tend to sickle and

55
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:28,620
get cleared, so carriers don't get the anaemia,

56
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:30,380
and they're much less likely to die of

57
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:31,340
severe malaria.

58
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:35,020
So where malaria is common, carriers survive and

59
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:37,820
have children more often, and the allele stays

60
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:38,460
common.

61
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:41,260
This is called heterozygote advantage.

62
00:02:42,467 --> 00:02:44,706
But two maps that line up aren't proof

63
00:02:44,747 --> 00:02:45,666
on their own.

64
00:02:45,907 --> 00:02:48,546
Two patterns can match by coincidence, just like

65
00:02:48,667 --> 00:02:49,666
any association.

66
00:02:50,227 --> 00:02:53,746
Scientists trust the malaria explanation because other studies

67
00:02:53,827 --> 00:02:57,186
showed directly that carriers really do resist severe

68
00:02:57,227 --> 00:02:57,986
malaria.

69
00:02:58,227 --> 00:03:00,986
And a high frequency can have another cause.

70
00:03:01,187 --> 00:03:03,826
In a small, isolated group, an allele can

71
00:03:03,907 --> 00:03:06,546
become common just by chance, because a few

72
00:03:06,627 --> 00:03:08,466
of the founders happened to carry it.

73
00:03:09,667 --> 00:03:12,066
The same kind of data helps protect threatened

74
00:03:12,147 --> 00:03:13,066
species.

75
00:03:13,347 --> 00:03:16,146
Tasmanian devils are being killed by a contagious

76
00:03:16,227 --> 00:03:17,586
facial cancer.

77
00:03:17,667 --> 00:03:20,146
Genetic data from many devils showed they have

78
00:03:20,227 --> 00:03:23,986
very little genetic diversity, meaning few different alleles,

79
00:03:24,067 --> 00:03:27,426
after past population crashes and isolation.

80
00:03:27,547 --> 00:03:30,626
That matters, because with fewer alleles there's less

81
00:03:30,707 --> 00:03:32,986
chance that some animals carry one that helps

82
00:03:33,027 --> 00:03:34,866
against a new disease.

83
00:03:34,947 --> 00:03:37,186
So breeders use the data to pair devils

84
00:03:37,267 --> 00:03:39,986
that are less related, keeping as much diversity

85
00:03:40,067 --> 00:03:40,906
as possible.

86
00:03:41,187 --> 00:03:43,746
But diversity only improves the odds.

87
00:03:43,827 --> 00:03:45,746
It doesn't guarantee survival.

88
00:03:46,967 --> 00:03:50,006
Large data also show how human populations are

89
00:03:50,327 --> 00:03:50,966
related.

90
00:03:51,287 --> 00:03:54,486
The 1000 Genomes Project sequenced the genomes of

91
00:03:54,567 --> 00:03:59,526
2,504 people from 26 populations around the world.

92
00:03:59,847 --> 00:04:02,646
Populations that share more variants are likely to

93
00:04:02,767 --> 00:04:04,766
share more recent ancestors.

94
00:04:05,127 --> 00:04:08,486
African populations carry the most variation, and most

95
00:04:08,567 --> 00:04:11,286
common variants found elsewhere are also found in

96
00:04:11,367 --> 00:04:11,926
Africa.

97
00:04:12,567 --> 00:04:15,766
That fits modern humans first evolving in Africa:

98
00:04:15,847 --> 00:04:17,686
each group that left was a small set

99
00:04:17,767 --> 00:04:21,086
of founders, carrying only part of that variety.

100
00:04:21,447 --> 00:04:24,806
And the reference genome isn't everyone's genome: a

101
00:04:24,887 --> 00:04:27,126
typical person differs from it at four to

102
00:04:27,247 --> 00:04:28,726
five million places.

103
00:04:29,934 --> 00:04:33,853
So, large data improve pattern detection, confidence in

104
00:04:33,934 --> 00:04:38,013
broad trends, and comparisons across many populations.

105
00:04:38,094 --> 00:04:40,893
What they don't remove is a biased sample,

106
00:04:40,974 --> 00:04:43,693
the limits of the methods, or uncertainty about

107
00:04:43,814 --> 00:04:45,773
any one person's future.

108
00:04:45,854 --> 00:04:49,213
UK Biobank volunteers, for example, are healthier on

109
00:04:49,454 --> 00:04:51,533
average than the British population.

110
00:04:51,614 --> 00:04:54,013
A strong answer states both sides.

111
00:04:55,234 --> 00:04:56,593
Two lines to remember.

112
00:04:57,154 --> 00:04:59,953
Big data sharpen the trend, but they don't

113
00:05:00,034 --> 00:05:01,273
predict a person.

114
00:05:01,554 --> 00:05:03,953
And if a harmful allele is common, look

115
00:05:04,034 --> 00:05:06,993
for a reason: an advantage for carriers, or

116
00:05:07,154 --> 00:05:08,593
chance in a small group.

117
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:10,920
Two questions now.

118
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:14,520
First, this table shows example data from a

119
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:17,640
large collaborative study: how common a red blood

120
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:20,680
cell variant is, and how much malaria there

121
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:22,200
is, in six regions.

122
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:23,800
Describe the trend.

123
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:27,720
Then suggest one reason the island region doesn't

124
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:28,120
fit.

125
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:29,960
Pause here to answer.

126
00:05:40,367 --> 00:05:43,566
The variant gets more common as malaria increases,

127
00:05:43,687 --> 00:05:46,206
which suggests carriers have an advantage where there's

128
00:05:46,287 --> 00:05:46,846
malaria.

129
00:05:47,327 --> 00:05:49,486
That's a trend, not proof.

130
00:05:49,807 --> 00:05:52,046
The island has a high frequency but no

131
00:05:52,127 --> 00:05:55,326
malaria, so something else is going on, like

132
00:05:55,367 --> 00:05:57,326
a few founders who happened to carry it.

133
00:05:57,967 --> 00:05:59,886
And if you said the variant must protect

134
00:06:00,047 --> 00:06:02,766
against malaria because the numbers match, a match

135
00:06:03,047 --> 00:06:04,766
alone doesn't show cause.

136
00:06:05,967 --> 00:06:06,526
Second.

137
00:06:07,007 --> 00:06:09,566
A threatened wallaby lives on the mainland and

138
00:06:09,647 --> 00:06:11,046
on a small island.

139
00:06:11,327 --> 00:06:14,766
Across ten markers, the mainland animals average nine

140
00:06:14,847 --> 00:06:17,806
alleles per marker, and the island animals average

141
00:06:17,967 --> 00:06:18,726
three.

142
00:06:19,087 --> 00:06:22,366
What does this suggest about the island population?

143
00:06:22,527 --> 00:06:25,606
Then name one way managers could use this.

144
00:06:25,887 --> 00:06:27,166
Pause here to answer.

145
00:06:37,567 --> 00:06:40,886
The island animals have much less genetic diversity.

146
00:06:41,167 --> 00:06:44,126
That suggests a past population crash, or a

147
00:06:44,207 --> 00:06:47,406
small founding group, with little mixing since.

148
00:06:47,727 --> 00:06:50,206
Managers could move some mainland animals to the

149
00:06:50,287 --> 00:06:53,246
island to add diversity, or pair the least

150
00:06:53,447 --> 00:06:55,166
related animals to keep what's left.

151
00:06:55,647 --> 00:06:58,206
And if you said more diversity guarantees they'll

152
00:06:58,287 --> 00:07:01,086
survive, it only improves their chances.

153
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:05,180
So, with any large data set, read the

154
00:07:05,260 --> 00:07:08,220
trend, ask what else could explain it, and

155
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:10,700
say what the data can't tell you.

156
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:13,180
Next, we'll look at what these population patterns

157
00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:16,780
can, and can't, predict about one person's risk.

158
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:19,440
Here are the key notes.

159
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:21,600
Pause the video if you need more time

160
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:22,480
to copy them.
